00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.100
Most women weren't allowed to work,

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:03.070
and thus sought financial
stability via marriage,

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:07.240
while men were incentivized
by that sweet, sweet dowry.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:08.750
- What's wrong with her?

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:13.750
She's beautiful, she's
rich, she's got huge

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:14.840
tracts of land.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:17.030
- [Michael] Courtship was
largely a private enterprise

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:18.600
arranged between families,

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:22.090
with women essentially
functioning as bartering tools

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:24.180
to solidify social alliances.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.920
Potential candidates were
evaluated based on class

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:28.120
and social status.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:31.680
Sociologist Eva Illouz also
notes that most young people

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.870
were pressured to accept
their first reasonable option.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:36.650
Love was nice if it happened,

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:39.750
but this was mostly a
regimented transaction.

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:42.263
- Not all of us can afford to be romantic.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:46.333
I've been offered a comfortable
home and protection.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:48.940
That's a lot to be thankful for.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:52.350
- Charlotte.
- I'm 27 years old.

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.620
I've no money and no prospects.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.280
- [Michael] But then,
in 1840, Queen Victoria

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:00.480
and legendary Wife Guy Prince
Albert entered the chat.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.599
- For me, this is not a
marriage of convenience.

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:04.889
(dramatic music)

00:03:04.889 --> 00:03:05.722
- No.

00:03:08.119 --> 00:03:10.210
I think it will be a
marriage of inconvenience.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:12.840
- [Michael] They were famously
head-over-heels in love,

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.890
a romance that got only more legendary

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:17.150
when Victoria publicly mourned Albert

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.900
for 40 years after his death.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:21.470
Royals, as you may know,

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:23.900
were basically the original influencers.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.380
So this romance, coupled
with the mainstreaming

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.430
of Enlightenment ideals
about personal fulfillment,

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:31.780
helped make marrying for romantic love

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:35.020
start to seem important, and even noble.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:37.710
For upper-class women,
this era of courtship

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:39.850
meant suitors leaving calling cards

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.340
and arranging chaperoned meetings.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:45.480
Like Bumble, but your frowning
step-mom is also there,

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.290
just sitting and judging.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.860
What's important is that
courtship now entailed

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:53.140
assessing compatibility between families,

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:54.730
personalities and so on,

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:57.220
rather than just settling
for the first Joe Schmo

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:58.990
who threw a stone at your window.

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:02.320
Working-class women, somewhat
empowered by the wage economy,

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.120
were less bound by ceremony,

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.440
and often lived in apartments too small

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.350
for men to come calling anyway.

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:10.910
They met men on their
way to work, in shops,

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:12.400
or at music halls.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.770
They dated, had sex,
juggled a bevy of admirers,

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:19.080
and it wasn't uncommon to be
pregnant on your wedding day.

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:21.580
Dating around wasn't just a fun time,

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:24.790
it was also considered essential
to finding the right match

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:26.180
in what Illouz calls

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.760
the, "Emergence of a self-regulated
market of encounters."

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.030
But here's the thing.

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:32.560
In the dating culture that emerged

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.210
at the end of the 19th century,

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:36.680
money became even more central

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:38.680
to the direct interactions of the couple,

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.610
because now dating meant going on dates,

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:44.510
and going on dates tends to cost money.

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:46.397
As scholar Beth L. Bailey explains,

00:04:46.397 --> 00:04:49.047
"Courtship was largely
construed and understood

00:04:49.047 --> 00:04:52.770
"in models and metaphors of
modern industrial capitalism."

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:56.170
As dates moved from living
rooms into dance halls and bars,

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:57.620
money was what Bailey calls

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:00.070
a, "Symbolic currency of exchange,"

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:03.260
where men needed to pay to
gain access to women's company.

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:07.107
By the 1920s, she notes,
"Dating provided a new frontier

00:05:07.107 --> 00:05:09.540
"for public competition
through consumption."

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:10.870
As a result, young people

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:13.910
started defining
themselves as commodities:

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:15.540
men by how much they could afford

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:17.160
to spend on theater tickets,

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:20.120
women by how much they
were worth spending on.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:23.050
This fittingly coincided
with the explosive growth

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:24.250
of the consumer economy,

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:27.260
which transformed ideals of romantic love.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:30.500
If men now needed to have money
to burn on vaudeville shows,

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:33.890
women now needed to look
like pre-war Kardashians.

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:36.780
That's in large part due to
the increased sexualization

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.070
of women in the media and advertising.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:41.730
Industries like cosmetics
relied on enticing imagery

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:43.730
to create demand for new products.

00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:46.400
As Illouz notes, before the 1920s,

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:49.770
the word sexy was actually
considered derogatory.

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:52.250
It was more preferable to
shack up with somebody who was,

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:54.880
say, super moral or reliable.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:57.260
But as mass media began pumping out images

00:05:57.260 --> 00:06:00.580
of eroticized female bodies,
and, soon enough, male bodies,

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.330
sexual attraction began
figuring into people's choices

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:05.680
in a much bigger way.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:08.700
As marital selection became
increasingly less based

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:11.520
on your social status and
more on subjective things

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:15.400
like sex appeal, the ability
to land a regulation hottie

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.760
started factoring more
into people's conceptions

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:19.730
of individual self worth.

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:21.737
As Illouz puts it, under modernity,

00:06:21.737 --> 00:06:24.790
"Sexuality thus becomes
closely intertwined

00:06:24.790 --> 00:06:26.050
with social value."

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:28.590
But of course, nothing
spoils the sex party

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:30.640
quite like a pair of world wars.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:32.240
Couples were split apart,

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:34.460
with courtship relegated
out of the dance hall

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.220
and back to the post office.

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:38.500
But it went beyond these basic logistics.

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:41.390
The idea of a chaste woman who stayed home

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:44.920
loyally awaiting the troops'
return became hugely popular,

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:46.800
especially during World War I.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:48.810
And that chaste woman became something

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:52.010
of a commodified trophy for
men down in the trenches

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:54.028
to fantasize and sing about.

00:06:54.028 --> 00:06:57.245
♪ My girl back home ♪

00:06:57.245 --> 00:07:00.167
♪ I'd almost forgot ♪

00:07:00.167 --> 00:07:03.038
♪ A blue-eyed kid ♪

00:07:03.038 --> 00:07:05.850
♪ I liked her a lot ♪

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:07.380
- While the fellas were away,

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:10.050
working-class and middle-class
women also spent more time

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:13.940
in the public sphere, doing
man stuff like going to job

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:16.470
and sometimes wearing trousers.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:20.360
But especially after World War
II, the return of the troops,

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:24.160
plus rising pressure to marry
early, pushed a lot of women

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:25.890
back into the home.

00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:28.630
Some of the rush to get
married was out of anxiety

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:31.300
about the scarcity of
eligible men post-war,

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:33.920
which reinforces a marketplace vibe.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:37.380
But this also had other
important economic implications.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:39.530
Whereas married women
had once been treated

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:41.260
like pure commodities,

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:44.670
housewives were now the main
consumers of commodities.

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:47.760
Men went to work, produced
goods, accumulated capital,

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:51.240
and their wives spent that
money buying more goods.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:54.230
Thus, the marriage industrial
complex became vital

00:07:54.230 --> 00:07:58.320
to keeping the consumption-driven
1950s economy humming.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:00.210
Meanwhile, for the burgeoning youth

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:02.200
consumer culture of the 1950s,

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:05.320
going steady became a
coming-of-age dating ritual

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:07.530
in its own right,
complete with varsity pins

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:11.319
and a sexy game called
Two Straws, One Milkshake.

00:08:11.319 --> 00:08:12.329
♪ Did they really get pinned ♪

00:08:12.329 --> 00:08:13.195
♪ Going steady ♪

00:08:13.195 --> 00:08:14.028
♪ I was hoping they would ♪

00:08:14.028 --> 00:08:14.861
♪ Going steady ♪

00:08:14.861 --> 00:08:17.930
- By the mid-1950s, 1 in
10 middle school students

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:21.630
would go steady at least
once before turning 11.

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:24.700
Of course, I better than
doubled those stats myself.

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:26.910
Going steady is essentially the origin

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:29.000
for how we conceive of dating today,

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:31.140
in all its serial monogamy.

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:34.730
And make no mistake, just
as it was for their parents,

00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:38.310
consumption was inextricable
from this boom in young love.

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:41.190
Shopping and investment
became metaphors for dating.

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:44.550
You needed a car to neck in,
a class ring to exchange,

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:47.130
a letterman jacket to drape
over your date's shoulders,

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:49.940
records to dance to and
turntables to play them on,

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:52.230
meals at the malt shop, and so on.

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:55.280
And dating remains a
consumption-driven activity

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:58.300
to this day, even if we've
swapped banana splits

00:08:58.300 --> 00:08:59.600
for charcuterie boards,

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:01.690
and class rings for Netflix passwords.

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:04.950
Now, the 1960s and '70s,
as you probably know,

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:07.740
brought varying degrees
of free love and rebellion

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:09.720
against the previous decades' sexual

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:11.280
and romantic constraints.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:15.300
But by the 1980s, we saw a
return to conservative values

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:18.240
in pretty much every
element of American society.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:21.250
It's around this time that
the 1950s were reimagined

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:24.090
as a nostalgic utopia of happy families

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:26.670
born from people who'd
just lost their virginity.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:29.570
The free-market-obsessed
'80s also saw the ascendancy

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:30.800
of dating technology,

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:32.660
like computer-based dating services,

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:36.050
or the truly heinous video dating boom.

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:37.830
- Hi, I'm Maurice.

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:40.500
I'm an executive by day,
and a wild man by night.

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:43.117
- These companies
promised to help you, say,

00:09:43.117 --> 00:09:45.447
"Do comparison shopping of potential dates

00:09:45.447 --> 00:09:48.120
"from the comfort and
privacy of your own home."

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:50.820
Again, we see the language of capitalism

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:52.960
continuing to permeate dating.

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:55.250
And then, came the internet.

00:09:55.250 --> 00:09:57.090
Now, technically, the earliest version

00:09:57.090 --> 00:10:00.410
of computerized dating
came from Harvard in 1965,

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:03.040
when some students created Operation Match

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:06.330
a half-century before your
favorite sunscreen enthusiast

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:09.000
would start your least-favorite
social networking site

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:10.360
in hopes of getting laid.

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:12.830
This 75-question-long survey helped

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:14.640
tens of thousands of people

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:16.960
learn about well-suited
potential new mates

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:18.340
in its first year alone.

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:21.630
And its algorithmic match
process has since been emulated

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:23.180
by modern dating sites.

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:25.240
These companies also used the knowledge

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:28.260
gained from those questionnaires
to mine consumer data,

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:31.500
a practice that would only
grow in the years to come.

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:34.920
As the internet entered more
homes, companies like AOL

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:38.150
funneled people into chatrooms,
bulletin boards, and so on,

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:41.930
based on mutual interests,
which inevitably led to dating.

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:45.910
The first dating site,
Kiss.com, launched in 1994,

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:49.660
followed shortly thereafter
by Match.com in 1995.

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:53.230
And by 1998, Nora Ephron
made the world a better place

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:54.810
with the film "You've Got Mail."

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:56.620
Side note: we actually made a video

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:59.510
about how that delightful
film is secretly fascist,

00:10:59.510 --> 00:11:00.930
so check it out.

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:04.380
Now, early online daters
encountered plenty of stigma

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:06.370
and were often painted as desperate.

00:11:06.370 --> 00:11:08.570
But the more online our lives have gotten,

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:11.770
the more mainstream
online dating has become.

00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:16.770
By 2007, Americans
alone spent $500 million

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:18.440
on online dating services,

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:21.090
making it the
second-most-profitable sector

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:22.580
of the internet economy.

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:26.320
And we all know what
the highest industry is.

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:29.040
The one you have to open
up the anonymous browser

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:30.700
to go look at so it's not in your history

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:32.500
so that if your mom uses your computer

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:34.710
that things doesn't happen
again like it did that one time.

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:38.240
So it comes as no surprise
that online dating services

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:42.030
are expected to rake in over
3 billion dollars this year

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:43.550
in the United States alone,

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:46.810
especially in light of how a
cornucopia of new dating sites

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:49.440
and apps have cropped up,
boasting new innovations

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:51.940
like Tinder's gamified swiping interface

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:54.350
or Bumble's women-led dating model.

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:55.700
A benefit of online dating

00:11:55.700 --> 00:11:57.590
is the way it creates
opportunities for people

00:11:57.590 --> 00:12:00.210
from specific communities
to connect with each other:

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:03.250
LGBTQ+ folks, people in rural areas,

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:06.570
elderly or disabled people,
hell, even fly fishermen.

00:12:06.570 --> 00:12:09.040
It can also be a relatively
unthreatening way

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:10.030
to meet new people,

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:13.790
and sometimes to screen out
potentially-bad matches.

00:12:13.790 --> 00:12:15.430
But we see something else going on

00:12:15.430 --> 00:12:18.670
that we think does a lot to
explain why online dating

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:23.040
can feel so hard, and
occasionally, pretty depressing.

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:24.930
Now, we've talked about the intersection

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:28.690
between dating and economics,
from dowries to theatre dates

00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:30.830
to the consumer demand
for engagement rings

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:32.830
that cost a month's salary.

00:12:32.830 --> 00:12:35.550
But we'd argue that online
dating basically takes

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:37.960
the commodification of
the actual act of dating

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:39.870
that cropped up around
the turn of the century

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:41.580
and turned it up to eleven.

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:43.510
- Put it up to 11.
- 11, exactly.

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:44.343
- How?

00:12:44.343 --> 00:12:47.570
Well, as scholars Rebecca
D. Heino, Nicole B. Ellison,

00:12:47.570 --> 00:12:49.687
and Jennifer L. Gibbs
note in their article

00:12:49.687 --> 00:12:53.250
"Relationshopping," dating
sites are typically designed

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:55.260
like online shopping platforms,

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:58.120
evoking the sensation of
literally window-shopping

00:12:58.120 --> 00:12:59.260
for a match.

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:01.577
They write that, "The
functionality and design

00:13:01.577 --> 00:13:04.507
"of online dating sites
encouraged participants

00:13:04.507 --> 00:13:06.697
"to adopt a marketplace orientation

00:13:06.697 --> 00:13:09.380
"towards the online dating experience."

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:12.480
Now, that doesn't necessarily
make Tinder sinister,

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:14.440
but it could have real implications

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:17.370
for the kinds of relationships
you're most likely to find.

00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:20.610
In "Relationshopping," the
authors write that online dating

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:23.767
may encourage an attitude
where prospective partners are,

00:13:23.767 --> 00:13:26.357
"Commodified as products to be sold,

00:13:26.357 --> 00:13:28.970
"assessed, purchased, or discarded."

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:31.120
Being discarded is probably fine

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:33.360
if you were only looking
for a one-time hookup,

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:35.140
but it lends a depressing air

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:37.727
if you're in search of a
long-term relationship.

00:13:37.727 --> 00:13:39.620
Illouz notes that it also leads

00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:41.300
to hyper-critical assessments

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:44.517
that render, "Potential partners
commensurable, measurable,

00:13:44.517 --> 00:13:46.360
"and comparable with each other."

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:49.470
And if sexual attractiveness
became a big deal in the '20s,

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:52.030
it's only been magnified in importance,

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:54.640
because these apps
facilitate a lightning-fast

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:56.610
superficial decision making process

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:59.510
typically based on a
few flattering photos.

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:01.700
Online dating also massively expands

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:03.250
your pool of potential matches,

00:14:03.250 --> 00:14:05.420
creating a sense of interchangeability.

00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:07.370
How many Daniels can you match with

00:14:07.370 --> 00:14:10.780
before they all blend into
the same guy in cargo shorts?

00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:12.970
And this replicates the overall sensation

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:14.620
we have as consumers today,

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:16.980
where we're similarly empowered to choose

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:21.200
between a dizzying array
of 125 toothpaste brands.

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:23.620
What's more, in the
logic of online dating,

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:26.240
potential matches are
reduced and scrutinized

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:27.850
by isolated attributes,

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:31.060
making them easier to reject or discard.

00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:33.250
Research has found that
the ease of discarding

00:14:33.250 --> 00:14:35.950
online dating partners may even continue

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:37.420
into a relationship.

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:39.090
That's partly because of the lack

00:14:39.090 --> 00:14:41.100
of an integrated social environment,

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:42.940
i.e. shared friends or communities,

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:45.250
which makes it feel easier to ghost.

00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:48.167
Overall, Illouz argues,
"Internet dating sites

00:14:48.167 --> 00:14:51.597
"display the consumerist logic
of increasingly narrowing,

00:14:51.597 --> 00:14:53.377
"defining, and refining tastes,

00:14:53.377 --> 00:14:56.010
"and comparing among
alternative possibilities."

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:57.760
While it's obviously good to be picky

00:14:57.760 --> 00:14:59.810
when it comes to choosing
a long-term partner,

00:14:59.810 --> 00:15:03.290
applying consumerist logic
to actual human beings

00:15:03.290 --> 00:15:05.580
seems less picky, more icky.

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:07.530
And it's not just your potential dates

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:08.760
that are being commodified here.

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:11.210
The authors of
"Relationshopping" also note

00:15:11.210 --> 00:15:14.780
that online dating encourages
self-commodification

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:17.477
via a, "Tangible and explicit assessment

00:15:17.477 --> 00:15:19.847
"of one's own perceived desirability

00:15:19.847 --> 00:15:21.547
"in ways less likely to occur

00:15:21.547 --> 00:15:24.090
"with traditional
face-to-face communication."

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:26.510
That is to say, participants
in online dating

00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:29.180
are constantly receiving reams of data

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:32.330
that cause them to assess
their own objective value

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:33.460
in the dating world.

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:36.600
Which can feel pretty harsh.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:39.160
So what does this all say about us?

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:41.450
Well, modernity, Illouz explains,

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:44.220
has been all about the
rise of the rational

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:46.140
in just about every aspect of life,

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:48.610
from psychology to weight-loss fads.

00:15:48.610 --> 00:15:52.040
Online dating reflects the
rationalization of love,

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:54.590
where we're encouraged
to assess potential dates

00:15:54.590 --> 00:15:56.680
as laundry lists of attributes

00:15:56.680 --> 00:15:59.280
rather than messy, complex human beings.

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:01.730
The endless supply of consumer choices

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:04.800
that defines late capitalism
has been effectively mapped

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:06.630
onto human relationships.

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:08.510
While some of us may romanticize

00:16:08.510 --> 00:16:11.580
the 1950s model of going steady long-term,

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:13.990
the tech we're using to find relationships

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:16.640
may be counterproductive
in achieving that goal.

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:17.473
Think about it.

00:16:17.473 --> 00:16:20.340
After World War II, women
settled into marriage quickly

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:22.550
due to the scarcity of eligible men.

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:25.490
In contrast, the seeming
abundance of matches

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:27.710
provided by online dating might incline us

00:16:27.710 --> 00:16:31.060
to not settle down at all, or
to discard a potential mate

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:33.630
simply because there
might be a shinier object

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:35.250
just a click away.

00:16:35.250 --> 00:16:38.520
Don't get us wrong, exploring
your options is great.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:40.830
But when it's treated
like the same process

00:16:40.830 --> 00:16:42.580
as finding the right Netflix show,

00:16:42.580 --> 00:16:45.570
it starts to feel a little cynical?

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:47.950
And the commodification of human beings

00:16:47.950 --> 00:16:50.430
on dating sites goes even deeper.

00:16:50.430 --> 00:16:52.417
Hinge might brand itself as the,

00:16:52.417 --> 00:16:54.640
"Dating app designed to be deleted,"

00:16:54.640 --> 00:16:57.520
but it's actually in all
the apps' best interests

00:16:57.520 --> 00:17:00.660
to keep you scrolling
through face after face.

00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:04.200
And their casino-style
interfaces make it all too easy

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:06.980
to waste an evening doing just that.

00:17:06.980 --> 00:17:09.720
Ultimately, these companies
make a great deal of money

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:11.740
by collecting super valuable data

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:14.450
about some of the most
intimate aspects of your life.

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:16.590
After all, these aren't old friends

00:17:16.590 --> 00:17:18.440
interested in helping you find love,

00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:21.350
they're tech platforms
interested in earning a buck.

00:17:21.350 --> 00:17:22.500
Or a billion of them.

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:25.180
So it seems that dating has
become more complicated,

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:28.130
and more commodified, than ever before.

00:17:28.130 --> 00:17:31.370
Finding a mate, a prospect
once simply determined

00:17:31.370 --> 00:17:33.100
by your family's financial capital,

00:17:33.100 --> 00:17:35.450
is now an important means
of affirming your worth,

00:17:35.450 --> 00:17:38.060
of broadcasting your individual social

00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:39.500
and/or cultural capital.

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:42.380
Qualities like your sense
of humor or your great hair,

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:44.197
Illouz says, thus start to become,

00:17:44.197 --> 00:17:46.550
"Indexes of your inner value."

00:17:46.550 --> 00:17:48.470
In other words, if you
used to be able to blame

00:17:48.470 --> 00:17:50.280
your bankrupt dad for hurting your chances

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:52.880
of landing a wife, now
you're implicitly told

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:55.750
to blame yourself for
not being witty enough

00:17:55.750 --> 00:17:57.600
in your About Me section.

00:17:57.600 --> 00:17:59.840
So what are we supposed to do?

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:03.080
We're years off from designing
our perfect partner in a lab,

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:05.600
free-love communes are
harder and harder to find,

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:09.100
and sex robots can be hacked
and turned into murder-bots.

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:12.050
So are we stuck as self-aware commodities

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:13.580
in an ever-expanding marketplace

00:18:13.580 --> 00:18:16.370
of increasingly disposable
romantic products?

00:18:16.370 --> 00:18:18.950
Well, barring some kind
of cultural backlash,

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:21.640
online dating will probably keep becoming

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:24.570
increasingly intrinsic
to our courtship rituals.

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:26.400
We can't change that.

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:29.920
But maybe recognizing the
unusual historical moment

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:33.160
of dating that we're in can
help us be a little less hard

00:18:33.160 --> 00:18:35.510
on ourselves when the
process feels hopeless.

00:18:35.510 --> 00:18:38.020
And maybe thinking about how
weird it is to treat yourself

00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:40.740
like a commodity can help us
be a little less judgemental

00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:43.580
towards men who post photos
of themselves with tigers.

00:18:43.580 --> 00:18:44.810
'Cause at the end of the day,

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:48.010
we're all looking for some
iteration of the same thing:

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:51.290
human connection, whether
for a night or forever.

00:18:51.290 --> 00:18:52.730
Well, that and romantic partners

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:55.620
who look like they're auditioning
for a Tiger King biopic.

00:18:55.620 --> 00:18:57.550
But what do you guys think?

00:18:57.550 --> 00:18:59.010
Do you wish your romantic life

00:18:59.010 --> 00:19:01.060
was still determined by dowries?

00:19:01.060 --> 00:19:03.190
Is online dating killing romance?

00:19:03.190 --> 00:19:05.990
Or is shopping for dates
fun and empowering?

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:08.020
Let us know in the comments.

00:19:08.020 --> 00:19:10.310
Big thanks to our patrons
for all your support,

00:19:10.310 --> 00:19:12.820
and be sure to check out our podcast.

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:15.580
Hit that subscribe button like
it's a Superlike on Tinder,

00:19:15.580 --> 00:19:17.620
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:19:17.620 --> 00:19:19.610
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:19:19.610 --> 00:19:20.443
Later.

00:19:20.443 --> 00:19:23.159
(cheerful music)

00:19:32.748 --> 00:19:35.680
Shout out to all my soldiers
online dating actively.

00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:37.440
It sounds rough.

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:39.320
Bring back arranged marriage.

00:19:39.320 --> 00:19:40.170
Okay, here we go.

